// Signal markers — fixed convention, never vary this file.
// Relies on longEntry / shortEntry / exitSignal booleans defined by that
// day's generated rules block (inserted immediately before this fragment).
//
// Those three are STATE, not events. A threshold set like "close above EMA9
// and EMA9 above EMA21 and RSI above 48" describes being in an uptrend, which
// stays true for dozens of consecutive bars — plotting it directly marked 43%
// of a session's bars long and 98% exit, which is unreadable. So a marker is
// drawn only on the bar where a condition BECOMES true: one sustained move
// produces one signal instead of one per bar. Edge detection lives here rather
// than in the generated block so it cannot be regressed at generation time.
//
// CLOSED BARS ONLY. The rules block is re-evaluated on every tick of the
// forming candle, so without the isconfirmed gate a marker appears, slides and
// vanishes as the bar develops — there is nothing stable to act on. Pine sets
// barstate.isconfirmed on a bar's final tick only, so a signal prints once,
// when the candle closes, and never un-prints. Every historical bar is
// confirmed, so chart history is unchanged by this. The trade-off is explicit
// and intended: the marker describes the last CLOSED candle, which means the
// fill is the next bar's open.
mk_long = longEntry and not longEntry[1] and barstate.isconfirmed
mk_short = shortEntry and not shortEntry[1] and barstate.isconfirmed
mk_exit = exitSignal and not exitSignal[1] and barstate.isconfirmed
plotchar(mk_long, title="Long Entry", char="L", location=location.belowbar, color=color.green, size=size.tiny)
plotchar(mk_short, title="Short Entry", char="S", location=location.abovebar, color=color.red, size=size.tiny)
plotchar(mk_exit ? close : na, title="Exit", char="X", location=location.absolute, color=color.orange, size=size.tiny)
